From 097bf0d508074a88d849caa69b1efda0dc10e951 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim 'mithro' Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 00:24:03 +0930
Subject: [PATCH] hw/usb: add a USB/IP server device

usbip-server exports the QEMU USB device on its port over USB/IP
(https://docs.kernel.org/usb/usbip_protocol.html), the protocol Linux's
vhci-hcd ("usbip attach") and other USB/IP clients speak:

  -chardev socket,id=usbipchr,host=127.0.0.1,port=3240,server=on,wait=off
  -device usbip-server,id=usbip0,chardev=usbipchr
  -device usb-storage,bus=usbip0.0,drive=disk0

It is a sysbus device without MMIO or IRQ owning a one-port USB bus,
allowed as a dynamic sysbus device on the raspi machines and -M none
(a bus-less device would do, but -device ...,bus= only finds buses in
the sysbus tree).  The transport is a chardev, as for usbredir.

- OP_REQ_DEVLIST/OP_REQ_IMPORT answer with the device record.  Because
  vhci-hcd never sends SET_ADDRESS or a port reset, the server
  enumerates the device first: port reset, SET_ADDRESS, device and
  configuration descriptors (with real control transfers, so a device
  answering asynchronously works too).
- CMD_SUBMIT becomes a USBPacket: control (setup in p->parameter),
  bulk, interrupt, and isochronous (one packet per ISO descriptor; an
  URB is ISO when number_of_packets >= 1 -- vhci sends 0 otherwise, the
  protocol document 0xffffffff).  Completions may be synchronous,
  asynchronous (many URBs in flight, per-endpoint order), or NAKed: a
  NAKed URB waits, retried on the device's wakeup and every millisecond.
- CMD_UNLINK cancels: -ECONNRESET if the URB was pending (no RET_SUBMIT
  follows), 0 if it had completed.  Status values are Linux errno
  numbers on every host.
- One client at a time.  A malformed message closes that connection
  only; device unplug and machine reset close it too, which is how a
  USB/IP exporter reports a removed device (as Linux's usbip-host does).

USBBus gains no_auto_hub: usb_claim_port() chains a usb-hub onto a bus
when a device takes its last free port, which on a one-port bus would
export the hub instead of the device.

Tested with a Python USB/IP client against usb-storage (descriptors,
BOT INQUIRY/READ CAPACITY/READ(10) of 64 KiB/WRITE(10) persisted to the
image), usb-kbd (interrupt IN held until a key press, unlink of pending
and completed URBs) and usb-audio (ISO OUT per-packet status, stalls on
a disabled stream), plus reconnection, malformed input, device_del and
system_reset.

